  "text": "To find the sum of the y-coordinates of the intersection points between the graph of $f(x)$ and the parabola $x=34y^2$, we need to solve the system of equations:\n$$f(x)=34y^2$$\n$$f(x)=x\\text{ if }x\\in[-1,1)$$\n$$f(x)=2-x\\text{ if }x\\in[1,3)$$\nWe can break the problem into two cases:\n1. When $x\\in[-1,1)$, we have $f(x)=x$, so we get $x=34y^2$. Solving for $y$, we get $y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}}$. Since $x\\in[-1,1)$, we have $y\\in[-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$.\n2. When $x\\in[1,3)$, we have $f(x)=2-x$, so we get $2-x=34y^2$. Solving for $y$, we get $y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{2-x}{34}}$. Since $x\\in[1,3)$, we have $y\\in[-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})\\cup[\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$.\n\nTo find the sum of the y-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to sum the y-coordinates of the points in each case. For the first case, we have two points: $(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$. The sum of the y-coordinates of these points is $2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}+2(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})=0$. For the second case, we have four points: $(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}},\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})$. The sum of the y-coordinates of these points is $2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}+2(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})+2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}+2(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})=0$.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the y-coordinates of the intersection points is $\\boxed{0}$.",
